# SOLID for DriftGuard

Apply SOLID to real variation seams. The architecture source is `DRIFTGUARD_PRD.md` sections 12 and 18.

## Single Responsibility (S)

- One reason to change per function, class, component, or module.
- Fastify routes validate and translate HTTP; orchestration services coordinate packages and repositories.
- Detector, lineage, adapter, repair policy, and validation packages each own one reason to change.
- React components render; hooks coordinate UI data; the API client calls the backend.

## Open/Closed (O)

- Extend with new functions or composed types — not inheritance hierarchies or plugin registries.
- New behavior = new code path behind existing seam, not a framework.

## Liskov Substitution (L)

- Test fakes and production implementations must honor the same contract, including errors, invariants, and lifecycle consistency.
- Breaking a port's contract is a bug — fix the implementation, not the caller's expectations.

## Interface Segregation (I)

- Small interfaces at demonstrated variation seams: `RepairModel`, persistent/in-memory repositories, and clocks when deterministic tests require one.
- No "god interfaces" with unused methods.

## Dependency Inversion (D)

- Core logic depends on contracts; application composition selects concrete model and repository implementations.
- Wire dependencies in `apps/api/src/app.ts` — no mutable global singletons outside the isolated mock-provider scenario store.
- **YAGNI gate:** no interface until a second implementation or test fake needs it.

## SOLID vs YAGNI

| SOLID says | YAGNI limits |
|---|---|
| Depend on abstractions at boundaries | Don't create abstractions before the boundary exists |
| One responsibility | Don't split one function into five files prematurely |
| Open for extension | Don't build extension points nothing extends yet |

When SOLID and YAGNI conflict, **YAGNI wins** until a second use case appears.
